p='content/security/01-fundamentals/secf-cia-aaa.md';q=p.replace('.md','.questions.json')
f='https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/FIPS/NIST.FIPS.199.pdf';h='https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html';n='https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html';l='https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html';nr='https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/non_repudiation';v='https://verisframework.org/attributes.html'
def e(a,b,why,s=[f]):edit(p,a,b,why,s)
def c(i,key,value,why,s,option=None):qe(q,'secf-cia-aaa-q'+str(i),key,value,why,s,option)
e('Information is **accurate and complete**, and unauthorised changes are prevented or at least detected.','Integrity protects information against **improper modification or destruction**. It does not prove that the original information was factually true.','Separate information integrity from truth.')
e('Signatures give you both.','A verified signature protects the signed bytes and links them to a signing key; attributing that key to a particular sender also requires a trusted key binding and protection of the private key.','Signature verification alone does not identify a human.',[nr])
e('Availability is the property security teams most often forget, and the one the business notices first. Ransomware is so damaging precisely because it attacks availability *and*, with data theft before encryption, confidentiality.','Ransomware can attack availability and integrity by altering files; if data is also stolen, confidentiality is affected too.','Remove unsupported frequency ranking and include integrity.')
e('You can trivially get perfect confidentiality: unplug the server and bury it. That destroys availability. Real design is about balance.','Disconnecting a server can reduce remote exposure while making its service unavailable. It does not prevent physical access or disclosure of existing copies. Real design evaluates the risks to each property.','Offline storage is not perfect confidentiality.')
e('- **Fail-closed** behaviour (deny on error) protects confidentiality; **fail-open** (allow on error) protects availability. A fire door fails open for a reason.','- **Fail-closed** behaviour (deny on error) can protect confidentiality and integrity while blocking legitimate use; **fail-open** (allow on error) can preserve access while bypassing a control. Choose based on the failure and threat model.','Remove incorrect fire-door analogy and qualify competing properties.')
e('> - Password reset tokens: confidentiality.\n> - A software update channel: integrity (a tampered update compromises every customer).\n> - An air-traffic control display: availability and integrity, far above confidentiality.\n> - A medical record: all three, which is why healthcare security is hard.','> Priorities depend on the system and threat model. For example:\n> - Password reset tokens: confidentiality, plus integrity of the account binding and expiry checks.\n> - A public software update channel: integrity and authenticity, so devices reject unauthorised releases.\n> - A time-critical operational display: availability and integrity can both be essential.\n> - A medical record: privacy, correct handling of changes and timely access all matter.','Remove universal impact rankings and fleet compromise guarantee.')
e('- **Non-repudiation**: someone cannot plausibly deny an action they took, such as signing a contract. This needs strong authentication plus tamper-evident records or signatures.','- **Non-repudiation**: evidence that supports attribution of an action and helps resolve a later dispute. Individual credentials, protected records and signatures can contribute; no mechanism alone guarantees attribution to a person or a legal outcome.','Avoid absolute and legal assurance claims.',[nr])
e("Donn Parker's **Parkerian hexad** adds possession (control), authenticity and utility. A ransomware gang that holds your encrypted backups breaks possession; a backup encrypted with a lost key has broken utility.","The VERIS framework uses paired attributes from the **Parkerian hexad**: confidentiality/possession, integrity/authenticity and availability/utility. Losing control of an encrypted drive can affect possession without exposing its plaintext; losing its only decryption key affects utility.",'Ground extension in primary implementing framework and separate control from decryption.',[v])
e('**authentication** (proving it)','**authentication** (checking evidence for that claim)','Credential control is evidence, not conclusive human identity.',[n])
e("The two are easily confused, and HTTP makes it worse: status `401 Unauthorized` actually means *unauthenticated* (we don't know who you are), while `403 Forbidden` means we know who you are and the answer is no.","HTTP `401 Unauthorized` means the request lacks valid authentication credentials for the target resource; the response must include a `WWW-Authenticate` challenge. `403 Forbidden` means the server understood the request but refuses it. A 403 can reflect insufficient permissions, but does not itself prove the requester was authenticated.",'Correct RFC semantics.',[h])
e('- **Non-repudiation and compliance**: proving who approved a payment.','- **Attribution and compliance evidence**: recording which account approved a payment.','Logs support evidence, not proof of human identity.',[l,nr])
e("Good audit logs are themselves an integrity problem. An attacker with admin rights will try to delete the logs that show them. So logs are shipped promptly to a separate system the attacker's credentials cannot reach, ideally append-only.","Good audit logs are themselves an integrity problem. Forward them promptly to a separately administered collector, ideally with append-only retention. This protects received records against production administrators, but a compromised source can still omit or forge events. Monitor collection failures and corroborate important events with other evidence.",'Separate preservation from source completeness.',[l])
e('**RADIUS** (RFC 2865) and its accounting extension','**RADIUS** (RFC 2865) and its accounting extension (RFC 2866)','Identify separate accounting RFC.',['https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2866'])
e('**Diameter** succeeded RADIUS in mobile networks.','**Diameter** (RFC 6733) is another AAA protocol, designed to address limitations of RADIUS and support applications including network access and mobility. This does not mean RADIUS has disappeared.','Avoid blanket replacement claim.',['https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6733'])
e('- The properties trade off: lockouts, encryption and fail-closed designs all protect one at another\'s expense.','- Controls can introduce trade-offs: evaluate key loss, lockout abuse and failure behaviour alongside the intended protection.','Trade-offs are potential, not inevitable.')
e('- Authentication proves identity, authorisation decides permission, accounting records what happened.','- Authentication checks evidence for a claimed identity, authorisation decides permission, accounting records events.','Avoid absolute identity proof.',[n])
e('- HTTP 401 means unauthenticated; 403 means authenticated but not authorised.','- HTTP 401 requires an authentication challenge; 403 is a refusal and does not guarantee authentication succeeded.','Keep summary consistent.',[h])
e('## Further reading','> [!note] Scope and evidence\n> These are illustrative threat models, not a universal ranking of risks. Legal assurance for signatures and production log completeness are not established here; those depend on the system, evidence and applicable process.\n\n## Further reading','Make unsupported assurances visibly absent.',[nr,l])
e('- [Information security — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_security)','- [NIST FIPS 199: security objectives](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/FIPS/NIST.FIPS.199.pdf)\n- [RFC 9110: HTTP status semantics](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html)\n- [OWASP: Logging Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [VERIS security attributes](https://verisframework.org/attributes.html)','Add fetched primary sources.',[f,h,l,v])
c(1,'workedExample','The stated modification is an integrity violation. The scenario does not establish an additional disclosure or service outage. A MAC or signature can detect changes to covered bytes when keys and verification are trustworthy; TLS protects the transport connection, not every later application action.','Avoid unconditional complete detection.',[f])
c(2,'explanation','The prompt already says the user is logged in. A 401 means this request lacks valid credentials for the target resource and must carry a challenge; a 403 alone does not establish login state.','Distinguish request credentials and user identity.',[h],0)
c(2,'explanation','Correct for this scenario: an authenticated user has been refused access. More generally, 403 does not imply successful authentication.','Correct status implication.',[h],2)
c(2,'workedExample','401: the request lacks valid authentication credentials for the target resource; include WWW-Authenticate.\n403: the server understood the request but refuses it, possibly for reasons unrelated to credentials.\nHere authentication is stated in the prompt, not inferred from the 403.','Correct answer derivation.',[h])
c(3,'explanation','A successful password check supplies evidence for the claimed account identity. Authorisation decides the permitted actions.','Qualify proof.',[n],1)
c(3,'explanation','Correct. The username claims an identity; checking the password supplies evidence for it.','Qualify proof.',[n],2)
c(3,'workedExample','Identification claims an account, authentication checks credential evidence, authorisation checks permissions, and accounting records events. Usernames are often public: do not rely on their secrecy as an authentication factor. Some identifiers can still be personal information deserving privacy protection.','Username public factor does not remove privacy.',[n])
c(4,'text','Availability; use account throttling with progressive delays, source/risk signals and a recovery path instead of this fixed 24-hour lockout','Source limits alone bypassable with distributed clients.',[n],1)
c(4,'explanation','Correct. The attacker can trigger the fixed lockout. Account throttling, progressive delays, source/risk checks and recovery can reduce the impact, but throttling still needs evaluation for denial-of-service risk.','Do not promise no lockout harm.',[n],1)
c(4,'workedExample','Five wrong guesses trigger a 24-hour denial of legitimate access. NIST gives increasing waits, for example 30 seconds up to an hour, as a possible mitigation alongside account attempt limits, bot challenges and risk signals. Per-IP limits alone are insufficient against distributed guessing. Stronger authentication and safe recovery help, but no throttle removes every availability trade-off.','Retain verified NIST example and account limits.',[n])
c(7,'workedExample','Forward logs beyond the production administration boundary and protect received records against deletion and alteration. A compromised source can suppress or forge events before collection, so preserved records are not necessarily a complete or truthful trail. Monitor forwarding failures and use independent evidence for important actions.','Collector protection does not assure source completeness.',[l])
c(8,'prompt','An update service distributes public firmware. Assume a short delay is tolerable but an unauthorised release would execute attacker code on devices that accept it. Which property is most critical in this scenario?','Make risk priority explicit.',[f])
c(8,'explanation','Correct. Integrity and authenticity checks help reject unauthorised releases before installation. Impact depends on what the device accepts and the privileges of the installed code.','No universal full privileges or fleet compromise.',[f],1)
c(8,'explanation','Availability matters, but this scenario explicitly tolerates a short delay and assigns higher impact to unauthorised code execution. Other systems can have different priorities.','Conditional priority.',[f],2)
c(8,'workedExample','The firmware is public, a short outage is acceptable, and accepting attacker code has high impact by assumption. Integrity and authenticity therefore take priority here. Release signatures require trustworthy verification keys and correct device-side checks; signing alone is not a complete update design.','Scenario based risk ranking.',[f,nr])
c(9,'prompt','Which choices can improve one CIA property while introducing a risk to another? Select all that apply.','Risk is potential not inevitable.',[f])
c(9,'explanation','Correct. Independent copies can improve availability and durability, but each location needs appropriate protection against disclosure. Confidentiality need not actually deteriorate if those controls are effective.','No automatic degradation.',[f],2)
c(9,'workedExample','A single-holder encryption key can improve secrecy while creating a recovery risk. A fail-closed firewall can preserve access policy while denying legitimate traffic during failure. Separate copies can improve recovery while adding locations to protect. These are risks to assess, not guaranteed losses. Fixing SQL injection has no inherent requirement to weaken another CIA property.','Qualify trade-offs.',[f])
c(10,'prompt','A company needs evidence tying approvals to individual accounts. Staff share one admin login and the audit log records only that account. What is the core problem?','Avoid absolute human/legal attribution requirement.',[nr])
c(10,'text','The shared login record alone cannot distinguish which staff member approved','External evidence could contribute; not impossible in principle.',[nr],1)
c(10,'explanation','Correct. The account identifier maps to several people. Individual credentials and protected records improve attribution, although they do not prove a human acted or that credentials were never compromised.','Qualify attribution.',[nr],1)
c(10,'workedExample','The log records admin, which maps to several staff. Individual accounts or individually controlled signing credentials improve the evidence, especially with strong authentication and protected records. A valid signature attributes bytes to a key under verification assumptions; legal effect and disputed human attribution require further evidence and are outside this lesson.','Remove unsupported strongest legal-grade claim.',[nr])
c(11,'explanation','Correct. Stop logging passwords, restrict access to exposed records, identify affected accounts and reset compromised credentials. Remove sensitive copies where feasible; immutable backups need controlled retention and handling rather than a promise of immediate universal deletion.','Deletion may be impossible; incident handling depends on storage.',[l],2)
c(11,'workedExample','The example writes a plaintext password into a more widely accessible store. Stop the logging, restrict access, determine the affected accounts, and reset exposed credentials. Redact or remove accessible sensitive records where feasible; handle retained or immutable backups through controlled retention and access. Historical incident examples are omitted here because their original disclosures were not retrieved for this verification.','Remove unverified historical specifics with explicit gap.',[l])
